### Batch 0.3 — Cloudflare Workers deploy pipeline

**Build:** Configure `wrangler.jsonc` with `main` and `assets.directory`, create-cloudflare already generated these. Add `pnpm deploy` script. Wire GitHub Actions to deploy on push to `main`.

**Important migration note:** `@astrojs/cloudflare` no longer supports Cloudflare Pages as of the current release — the team explicitly recommends Workers for new projects ([@astrojs/cloudflare docs](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/cloudflare/)). If the user already has a Pages project, follow [Cloudflare's Pages→Workers migration guide](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/migration-guides/migrate-from-pages/).

**Free-tier budget (verify against [Cloudflare Workers pricing](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/pricing/)):**
- 100 k requests/day free; 10 ms CPU/request on free, 30 s on paid.
- Unlimited static asset requests (don't count against Workers request count when served from Assets).
- No bandwidth cap ([agentdeals – hosting free tiers 2026](https://agentdeals.dev/hosting-free-tier-comparison-2026)).

**Vercel comparison:** Vercel Hobby = 100 GB bandwidth but **prohibits commercial use** ([agentdeals](https://agentdeals.dev/hosting-free-tier-comparison-2026)). For a personal portfolio, both are viable, but Cloudflare is lower-risk if the site ever gets monetized (e.g. affiliate links). Netlify moved to a credit-based free tier that's less predictable. **Keep Cloudflare Workers.**

**Acceptance tests:** Push to `main` causes live URL to update within 2 min; `curl -I` returns 200 + HTTP/3.

