# Phase 3 — Content Pipeline

**Goal:** Writing long-form MDX with interactive islands, embedded code execution, and optimized images.

**Exit criteria:** A single MDX file can: render a Svelte counter, run a live editable JS snippet, embed an optimized image, get syntax-highlighted code blocks with titles, and export to RSS with full content.

### Batch 3.1 — MDX + Expressive Code

**Build:** `@astrojs/mdx` integration + `astro-expressive-code` for code blocks. Enable titles, line highlighting, copy-to-clipboard, frame decoration.

**Libraries:**
- `@astrojs/mdx` ^ latest for Astro 6.
- `astro-expressive-code` latest ([expressive-code.com](https://expressive-code.com/releases/)) — bundles Shiki 4 under the hood (Shiki 4 is the default highlighter in Astro 6 via `<Code />` and markdown — [Astro 6.0](https://astro.build/blog/astro-6/)).

**Expressive Code vs plain Shiki vs Shiki Twoslash:**
- Expressive Code is **Shiki with a plugin ecosystem**: since the Shikiji merge, EC uses Shiki again but adds titles, line-markers, copy buttons, terminal framing, word-wrap ([EC release notes](https://expressive-code.com/releases/)). It is how the Astro docs site itself is built.
- Shiki Twoslash: best for TypeScript teaching blogs where you need inline type hovers. EC has a **community** Twoslash plugin but with caveats — EC's architecture is "not yet compatible with Shiki's transformers API," so most Shiki transformers silently break ([EC syntax highlighting docs](https://expressive-code.com/key-features/syntax-highlighting/)). The `expressive-code-twoslash` plugin exists ([withstudiocms repo](https://github.com/withstudiocms/expressive-code-twoslash)) but is niche. **Decision: skip Twoslash initially; add only if the user writes heavy-TS tutorials.**

**Spec:** default theme (a dual light/dark pair, e.g. `github-light` + `github-dark`, or Night Owl), terminal languages, copy button enabled, word-wrap default=off, no Twoslash.

**Acceptance tests:** a fixture `.mdx` with a JS code fence renders with title + copy button; `prefers-color-scheme` swaps theme live.

---

### Batch 3.2 — Embedded Svelte islands inside MDX

**Build:** A convention: place interactive components in `src/components/mdx/` and import them at the top of each MDX file (or via MDX `components` prop globally).

**Known compatibility notes:**
- Svelte 5 islands in MDX work in Astro 6. Recent fixes have landed for type checking and `$props.id()` uniqueness when the same Svelte island appears multiple times on a page ([svelte integration changelog](https://github.com/withastro/astro/blob/main/packages/integrations/svelte/CHANGELOG.md)).
- Use `client:visible` as default for heavy islands; `client:load` only when interactivity is needed pre-scroll.

**Spec:** hydration directive policy, how to pass props from MDX frontmatter to an island, how to embed a named Svelte island via a custom MDX component map.

**Acceptance tests:** two instances of the same Svelte island on one page have unique `$props.id()` values; state in one does not leak to the other.

---

### Batch 3.3 — Code execution in MDX

**Build:** A reusable `<Sandbox />` MDX component that renders a live-editable, runnable code playground.

**Recommendation: Sandpack for most cases; StackBlitz WebContainers only if full Node is needed.**

| Option | Cost | Language support | Offline | When to use |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Sandpack** (`@codesandbox/sandpack-react` or lower-level `sandpack-client`) | Free, open-source | JS/TS/React/Vue, static HTML/CSS, Node via Nodebox for select templates | Service-Workers-based; blocked by strict privacy settings ([Josh Comeau on Sandpack](https://www.joshwcomeau.com/react/next-level-playground/)) | Default: single-file or small multi-file snippets in articles. Smallest footprint. |
| **StackBlitz WebContainers** (`@webcontainer/api`) | Free for non-commercial / documented SDK | Full Node.js in browser, supports npm/pnpm/yarn ([WebContainers](https://sourceforge.net/software/product/StackBlitz-WebContainers/)) | Yes after initial load | Reach for only when an article needs a real npm install or multi-process Node demo. |
| Pyodide / QuickJS self-hosted | Free | Python / JS only | Yes | For /math pages with live Python, load Pyodide lazily on user action. |
| CodeSandbox iframe (legacy) | Free | Everything | No | Reject — ships a full third-party UI. |

**Important caveat on Sandpack:** it's a **React** component toolkit. To use inside Astro without dragging in React as a UI framework, either:
1. Use `<Sandpack client:only="react" />` as an island — adds React runtime (~40 KB gz) to pages that embed playgrounds.
2. Use the lower-level framework-agnostic `sandpack-client` bundler and drive it from Svelte. More work; no React runtime.

**Decision:** accept the React runtime for pages that include a sandbox (expected: <10 % of pages); use `client:only="react"` so it's isolated.

**Libraries:**
- `@codesandbox/sandpack-react` ([sandpack.codesandbox.io](https://sandpack.codesandbox.io/))
- `react` + `react-dom` (island-only)
- `@webcontainer/api` — optional, for dedicated tutorial pages

**Spec:** props (`template`, `files`, `dependencies`, `readOnly`), theme tokens matching Expressive Code, lazy-load via `client:visible`.

**Acceptance tests:** a sandbox renders and executes a React counter; typing re-runs within 500 ms; no console errors; page without a sandbox ships 0 bytes of React.

---

### Batch 3.4 — Image optimization

**Build:** Use Astro's built-in `<Image />` + `sharp` for authored images. For card thumbnails and blog hero images, use `getImage()` at build time.

**Libraries:** `astro:assets` built-in; `sharp` as peer dep (auto-installed).

**Cloudflare Images vs built-in:** Astro 6's Cloudflare adapter defaults `imageService` to `cloudflare-binding`, which transforms images at runtime via Cloudflare Images ([@astrojs/cloudflare docs](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/cloudflare/)). For a primarily-static site, set `imageService: 'compile'` to keep image processing at build time — no runtime Cloudflare Images spend.

**Spec:** allowed formats (avif + webp + png fallback), responsive sizes (`[320, 640, 960, 1280, 1920]`), `loading="lazy"` default, `fetchpriority="high"` for hero.

**Acceptance tests:** built HTML has `srcset`; Lighthouse LCP image is AVIF on modern browsers.

