# Phase 5 — Digital Garden (Obsidian Import)

**Goal:** Publish a filtered subset of the Obsidian vault as an interconnected garden with wikilinks, backlinks, and a browsable graph view.

**Exit criteria:** user marks a note `publish: true` in Obsidian frontmatter → it appears on site with working `[[wikilinks]]` and backlinks listing; the graph view renders all published notes.

### Batch 5.1 — Vault import pipeline

**Three options evaluated:**

| Approach | Pros | Cons | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| **(a) Symlink vault → `src/content/notes/`** | Live editing in Obsidian, zero copy step | Obsidian officially advises against symlinks (sync/corruption risk per [Obsidian docs](https://www.mandalivia.com/obsidian/obsidian-with-astro-for-personal-site/)); publishes everything by default | Too risky |
| **(b) `astro-loader-obsidian` or `starlight-obsidian`** | Existing community tooling; handles wikilinks, embeds, backlinks ([starlight-obsidian in use](https://notes.caro.fyi/notes/using-obsidian-with-astro-starlight/)) | Starlight plugins sometimes assume the Starlight shell; pure Astro loaders vary in maintenance | **Primary: try `astro-loader-obsidian` via the Content Layer `loader` API** |
| **(c) Hand-rolled Node script** (like [rachsmith.com's approach](https://rachsmith.com/automating-obsidian-to-astro/)) | Full control, filters on `publish: true`, rewrites wikilinks → markdown links, unpublished-target stripping | DIY maintenance | **Fallback: if loader plugin lacks a feature, ship a 150-LOC script that reads the vault, filters, transforms, writes `src/content/notes/**/*.md`** |

**Recommendation: (b) first, (c) as escape hatch.** The Astro team has added Obsidian vaults to the example community loaders list ([Astro Content Collections docs](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/content-collections/)), so `astro-loader-obsidian` is the canonical path. Verify it's active (commits within 3 months) before locking in; if stale, use (c).

**Spec:** vault root path, filter rule (`publish: true` frontmatter), slug rule (filename slugified + folder path optional), wikilink target resolution (exact filename match → slug; if missing, render as plain text not broken link).

**Acceptance tests:** a note with `publish: false` does not appear; a `[[foo]]` link to a published note becomes `<a href="/notes/foo">foo</a>`; a `[[bar]]` to unpublished note renders as bold plain text with a `data-unpublished` attribute.

---

### Batch 5.2 — Wikilink + embed transformation

**Build:** Remark plugin chain during MDX processing.

**Libraries:**
- `remark-wiki-link` or `remark-obsidian` (verify most-recently-maintained in April 2026 — the [Astro roadmap discussion](https://github.com/withastro/roadmap/discussions/437) confirms wikilinks still aren't native, so a plugin is required).
- `remark-obsidian-callout` for `> [!note]` blocks if used.
- `remark-math` + `rehype-mathjax` or `rehype-katex` for LaTeX (KaTeX is smaller, MathJax is more complete; pick **KaTeX** for file-size).

**Spec:** plugin order (wiki-link before math before highlight), Obsidian-style `![[image.png]]` embed → `<Image>` component, unresolved-link styling.

**Acceptance tests:** note containing `[[Philosophy of Mind]]`, `![[graph.png]]`, and `$\int x^2$` renders correct links, an optimized image, and KaTeX output.

---

### Batch 5.3 — Backlinks at build time

**Build:** An integration hook (`astro:build:done` or a custom step after collection sync) that walks every note, extracts outgoing links, inverts the map, and stores a JSON index in `src/data/backlinks.json`. Each note page reads its entry and renders a "Backlinks" footer.

**Recommendation: build-time, not client-time.** Client-time would require shipping the full link graph as JSON; build-time means a single sorted list per page is inlined server-side — no runtime JS required. Follows the pattern used by `starlight-site-graph` (which [Caro vendors in directly](https://notes.caro.fyi/notes/using-obsidian-with-astro-starlight/)).

**Spec:** canonical ID (slug), handling of orphan notes, alphabetical vs chronological sort of backlinks.

**Acceptance tests:** if note A links to note B, B's rendered HTML contains a link back to A in a `<section class="backlinks">`.

---

### Batch 5.4 — Hover link previews

**Build:** A Svelte island (`<LinkPreview>`) that on hover/focus of a `.wikilink` anchor, fetches the target page's summary + first paragraph from a small JSON index built alongside backlinks, shows a floating card.

**Acceptance tests:** hover over a wikilink shows a preview within 100 ms; respects `prefers-reduced-motion` by disabling animation; works with keyboard focus.

---

### Batch 5.5 — Graph view page

**Build:** `/garden/graph` with an interactive force-directed graph of all published notes.

**Library shortlist (April 2026):**

| Library | Renderer | Notes | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Sigma.js 3 + graphology** ([sigmajs.org](https://www.sigmajs.org/)) | WebGL | Designed for thousands of nodes; framework-agnostic; used by Caro's note site via starlight-site-graph; MIT | **Primary** |
| **Cosmograph** ([Neo4j overview](https://neo4j.com/blog/graph-visualization/neo4j-graph-visualization-tools/)) | WebGL | Renders 100 k+ nodes smoothly; CC-BY-NC for non-commercial, proprietary license for commercial | Good if the garden grows huge and is non-commercial |
| **D3-force + Canvas/SVG** | Canvas/SVG | Most flexible; slower at 5 k+ nodes; ubiquitous | Best if the user wants highly customized node/edge design |
| **Cytoscape.js** | DOM | Biological-graph origin; DOM-dependent so no multithreading ([Memgraph graph lib survey](https://memgraph.com/blog/you-want-a-fast-easy-to-use-and-popular-graph-visualization-tool)) | Reject — perf issue above ~2 k nodes |
| **vis-network** | Canvas | Popular but slowest of the three in head-to-head benchmarks ([Memgraph survey](https://memgraph.com/blog/you-want-a-fast-easy-to-use-and-popular-graph-visualization-tool)) | Reject |
| **react-force-graph** | WebGL | Great but drags React runtime in | Reject (framework mismatch) |

**Recommendation: Sigma.js 3 with graphology as the data model.** Falsifiable criterion: renders 5 000 nodes at 60 fps in Chrome on a 2020 MacBook Air (per [Memgraph's benchmark](https://memgraph.com/blog/you-want-a-fast-easy-to-use-and-popular-graph-visualization-tool)); framework-agnostic so it drops into a Svelte island cleanly. If graph stays under ~500 nodes and the user wants custom node art, revisit D3-force.

**Spec:** nodes = notes, edges = wikilinks; node size = log(backlink count); color by tag cluster (optional, via community detection from `graphology-communities-louvain`); zoom/pan/click→navigate; keyboard-accessible "list view" fallback.

**Acceptance tests:** renders a 500-node fixture at 60 fps; clicking a node navigates; full list of notes is keyboard-reachable (graph view is progressive enhancement on top of an accessible list).

